We want to compute statistics based on LADOK data.
We want to answer the following questions:
\begin{enumerate}
\item\label[question]{QGradeDistInCourse} What are the grade distribution of a 
course round?
\item\label[question]{QGradeDistDiffYears} How do grade distributions differ 
between years (\ie rounds) for the same course?
\item\label[question]{QGradeDistDiffCourses} How do grade distributions differ 
between (similar) courses?
\item How is the throughput distribution of a course?
\item How do throughput distributions vary between years?
\end{enumerate}

We provide the module [[data]] ([[<<data.py>>]]) to extract the necessary data
from LADOK through the command-line interface.
It provides two related subcommands:
\begin{description}
\item[ [[ladok course]] ] Export all results for one course.
\item[ [[ladok program]] ] Export the results for the students in one or more
  program rounds across the courses they actually took.
\end{description}
It can be used like this:
\begin{minted}{bash}
ladok course DD1315 > DD1315.csv
ladok program CTFYS --round HT25 > CTFYS-HT25.csv
\end{minted}
These commands produce CSV-formatted data to answer the questions above.
The [[course]] subcommand writes:
\begin{itemize}
\item course,
\item round,
\item component,
\item student (pseudonym),
\item grade,
\item either absolute date or normalized to the course start and finish.
\end{itemize}
The [[program]] subcommand prepends a first [[Program]] column whose value
identifies the cohort as the program code together with the program round code
and start year.


\section{The [[course]] and [[program]] subcommands}\label{DataCommand}

These subcommands run as part of the [[ladok3.cli]] module.
We provide a function [[add_command_options]] that adds their parser options to
a given parser.
We need access to LADOK through the [[ladok3]] module.
We will also write data to disk in CSV form, so we need the [[csv]] module.
<<[[data.py]]>>=
